    "https://careers.universiteitleiden.nl/job/PhD-position-PFAS-free-membranes-for-use-in-electrolysers/16951-en_US": {
      "title": "PhD position: PFAS free membranes for use in electrolysers",
      "description": "PhD position: PFAS free membranes for use in electrolysers Job Details | Universiteit Leiden We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies Home Vacancies Job Description PhD position: PFAS free membranes for use in electrolysers Job requisition Id: 16951 Job type: PhD positions Contract hours min: 38 Contract hours max: 38 Location: Leiden Applying is possible until: 20 November 2026 Apply now Why apply? This multidisciplinary project integrates electrochemistry, metal–organic framework-based materials, and membrane chemistry to develop the next generation of high-performance membranes and unlock new possibilities for advanced molecular separation and transport. Can you design the membrane that powers the next generation of electrolysers? Electrolysis and electrocatalysis have a key role to play in creating a more sustainable chemical industry. At the heart of these technologies are membranes that control the transport of protons and other species—but today’s proton exchange membranes rely heavily on PFAS-based materials such as Nafion®. We are looking for a PhD candidate to develop a new generation of high-performance, PFAS-free proton-conductive membranes. In this project, you will combine the unique properties of proton-conductive metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with those of proton-conductive polymers to create innovative membrane materials. You will systematically investigate how molecular structure, composition, and membrane architecture determine proton transport and overall performance. Your research will span materials chemistry, membrane science, and electrochemistry. You will establish design principles for high-performance proton-conductive membranes and put your materials to the test in different electrolyser configurations. This is an opportunity to work at the interface of fundamental materials science and real-world energy technology, with the ambition to develop membrane materials that can help enable a cleaner and more sustainable chemical industry. The PhD students will be supervised by Dennis Hetterscheid (Leiden Institute of Chemistry). What you bring We are looking for an enthusiastic and motivated candidate who is excited to pursue an interdisciplinary PhD at the interface of electrochemistry, materials chemistry, and membrane science. The project combines electrochemistry, MOF synthesis and characterization, physical measurements, and electrolysis studies, offering the opportunity to develop expertise across multiple fields. You hold an MSc degree in Physical Chemistry, Chemistry, Materials Science, or a related discipline, with a background in one or more of the following areas: electrochemistry, membrane chemistry, MOF synthesis, or materials characterization. Most importantly, you are curious, eager to learn, and motivated to work across disciplinary boundaries. You will join a highly collaborative and international research team of PhD candidates, postdoctoral researchers, MSc students, and scientific staff. We collaborate extensively with research groups both locally and internationally, and work closely with industry partners to translate fundamental scientific discoveries into technologies with real-world societal impact. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone can thrive. Our lab values diversity, accessibility, and inclusion in all their forms, and we strive to foster a safe and supportive workplace that encourages personal and professional growth, well-being, scientific integrity, and responsible research. If you are excited by the opportunity to combine fundamental materials chemistry with electrochemical energy technologies and help develop the membranes of the future, we would love to hear from you. Skills: Hands-on experimental experience , with co-authorship of one or more scientific publications considered an advantage. A strong interest in fundamental science and a genuine curiosity about understanding the principles underlying new materials and technologies. High motivation for research , combined with a solid theoretical understanding of relevant scientific principles. Strong organizational and time-management skills , with the ability to plan your work, work independently, and communicate your progress clearly. Excellent written and spoken English . A collaborative mindset and the ability to work effectively as part of a diverse, interdisciplinary team. What we offer Our goal is to work together to create a transparent and inclusive work environment in which everyone feels welcome and appreciated. Our organization is always evolving and we need your ideas for improvement and innovation to take us further. We want to devote attention to your personal development. You can count on an enjoyable job within the socially relevant world of education and research. The University's challenging and international work environment is located just steps away from Leiden’s lively city centre or the bustling city centre of The Hague. We also want to work with you to devote attention to your health and vitality, for example with the fun activities we organize through Healthy University. The position will be for a maximum of 4 years, and the candidate is expected to complete a PhD thesis at the end of this period. A contribution to classroom teaching (maximum 10% of the time) will be part of the responsibilities of the candidate, as well as supervision of BSc and MSc research projects that contribute to the goals of this PhD project. We offer a stimulating and interactive research environment, free use of all state-of-the-art facilities, and a competitive salary. We also offer: An employment contract for 12 months initially, to be extended for 3 more years upon satisfactory performance. This contract falls under the CLA of Dutch Universities; A salary between € 3.204,- and  € 4.051,-  gross per month, based on a full-time appointment (38 hours - PhD student); A holiday allowance (8%), an end-of-year bonus (8,3%), and an attractive pension scheme at ABP; Full reimbursement of public transport commuting costs for home-to-work travel; Flexible working hours: as a standard, you are entitled to a minimum of 29 leave days on the basis of a full-time working week of 38 hours; you can also save for extra leave, for example by working 40 hours a week, and in this way accrue an extra 96 leave hours, or exchange 96 leave hours for a 36-hour week. Lots of options when it comes to secondary employment conditions; we can, for example, discuss options for paid parental leave. Within our terms of employment individual choices model, you can exchange leave days and/or salary for benefits such as an advantageous sports subscription and bicycle scheme; If your work allows it, hybrid working is possible within the Netherlands; A home-working allowance (day and internet allowance) and attention for good workplaces. For more information about employment conditions, see https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/working-at/job-application-procedure-and-employment-conditions Where you will work Leiden University is one of Europe’s leading international research universities. Thanks to its committed and inspired employees, the university enjoys a leading role in academic research and education. Every year, Leiden University scores very well in prominent rankings. This means that, to maintain our position, the bar is set very high. Regardless of the work you do at our university, you are always encouraged to broaden your horizons, develop your talents and reach your full potential. Faculty of Science & Leiden Institute of Chemistry The Faculty of Science at Leiden University is a world-class faculty where staff and students work together in a dynamic international environment. It is a faculty where personal and academic development are top priorities. Our people are committed to expand fundamental knowledge by curiosity and to look beyond the borders of their own discipline; their aim is to benefit science, and to contribute to addressing the major societal challenges of the future. The research carried out at the Faculty of Science is very diverse, ranging from mathematics, information science, astronomy, physics, chemistry and bio-pharmaceutical sciences to biology and environmental sciences. The research activities are organized in eight institutes. These institutes offer eight bachelors’ and twelve master’s programmes. The faculty has grown strongly in recent years and now has more than 2,300 staff and over 5,000 students. We are located at the heart of Leiden’s Bio Science Park, one of Europe’s biggest science parks, where university and business life come together. The research is embedded within the Leiden Institute of Chemistry, with its long-standing traditions of excellence in research. Apart from a state-of-the-art electrochemistry laboratory, and excellent synthesis facilities, preparation and measurement facilities, unconventional nanofabrication equipment and experimental setups to measure ion transport will be designed supported by outstanding mechanical and electronic workshops. We foster an international and collaborative atmosphere and have close ties to other institutes housed within our Faculty of Science and with many research groups within the Netherlands, and many prominent labs abroad. What we value Promoting an inclusive community is central to Leiden University’s values and vision. We strive to be an open community in which all students and staff members feel valued and respected, and are able to fully develop themselves. We consider an inclusive academic environment, where students and staff can share their experiences and viewpoints, to be essential for the development of talent. Want to apply or find out more? If you want to apply straight away, click the application button. If you would like more information about what the job entails, please contact Dr. Dennis Hetterscheid, at d.g.h.hetterscheid@chem.leidenuniv.nl. You can apply until 20 November 2026; You will receive a response to your application no later than two weeks after the vacancy closing date. Your application should include a motivation letter clearly indicating WHY you apply for this position and project and WHAT makes you a suitable candidate, an extensive CV, the courses you took and grades you obtained in your Master’s studies, and the names and contact details of at least two references. You will receive a response to your application no later than two weeks after the vacancy closing date. If you have any questions about the procedure, please contact our management- & office assistent Angelo Philippi ( a.philippi@lic.leidenuniv.nl ). To help us get to know each other better, we follow a number of steps in the application procedure. For more information, see https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/working-at/job-application-procedure-and-employment-conditions Apply now Information at a Glance Faculty / Unit: The Faculty of Science Contact with (Name + position): Dr. Dennis Hetterscheid Contact Email address: d.g.h.hetterscheid@chem.leidenuniv.nl Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Study programmes Bachelor's programmes Master's programmes PhD programmes Education for Professionals Summer Schools Other modes of study Bachelor's Open Days Master's Open Days Admission & Application Organisation Archaeology Governance and Global Affairs Humanities Law Medicine/LUMC Science Social and Behavioural Sciences African Studies Centre Leiden Honours Academy ICLON (Graduate School of Teaching) About us Working at Leiden University Support Leiden University Alumni Impact Leiden-Delft-Erasmus Universities Locations Disclaimer Cookies Privacy Contact × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data center \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary)  prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Functional Cookies These cookies provide a better customer experience on this site, such as by remembering your login details, optimizing video performance, or providing us with information about how our site is used. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionalities that these third-parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Functional Cookies Show More Details Functional Cookies Provider Description Enabled YouTube YouTube is a video-sharing service where users can create their own profile, upload videos, watch, like, and comment on videos. Opting out of YouTube cookies will disable your ability to watch or interact with YouTube videos. Cookie Policy Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider YouTube Confirm My Choices Accept All Cookies ​ ​"
    },
    "https://careers.universiteitleiden.nl/job/Postdoctoral-researcher%2C-Computational-linguistics%2C-morphology-and-constructicography-of-Dutch/16898-en_US": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral researcher, Computational linguistics, morphology and constructicography of Dutch",
      "description": "Postdoctoral researcher, Computational linguistics, morphology and constructicography of Dutch Job Details | Universiteit Leiden We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies Home Vacancies Job Description Postdoctoral researcher, Computational linguistics, morphology and constructicography of Dutch Job requisition Id: 16898 Job type: Academic staff Contract hours min: 28 Contract hours max: 40 Location: Leiden Applying is possible until: 1 November 2026 Apply now Faculty of Humanities, Leiden University Centre for Linguistics Postdoctoral researcher, Computational linguistics, morphology and constructicography of Dutch Starting date: January 2027 (a later starting date can be negotiated) Deadline : November 1, 2026 Description Leiden University Centre for Linguistics (LUCL) invites applications for a Postdoc position within the NWO project ‘The Morphicon: A new gateway to morphological knowledge’. The postdoctoral researcher will work in a small team to build a morphological constructicon for Dutch , and to use it to advance our understanding of productivity in word-formation . Constructicons are digital networks of grammatical constructions in a language. Such resources are being built for a variety of major languages, but not yet for Dutch. Moreover, existing constructicons all focus on syntactic constructions, neglecting morphology. The aim of this project is to develop the first morphological constructicon or “Morphicon” with Dutch as the target language. The resource will be developed by a team of three researchers—the PI, the Postdoc and a PhD—in collaboration with the Instituut voor de Nederlandse Taal. The postdoctoral researcher will participate in the team effort, but will also have their own research project. The team will work together on transforming existing lexicographic and grammatical resources into a digital network. The network will consist of lexical constructions (simple and complex words) and grammatical constructions (word-formation patterns), linked to one another by the properties they share. An important part of the work will be to find, test and develop digital tools to assist in classification, annotation and network building. The Postdoc, the computational expert in the team, will play a key role in this process. The Morphicon provides essential empirical data relevant for the investigation of linguistic productivity, including word-formation patterns of varying generality, information on semantic and formal hubs and outliers, competing affixes and frequency distributions. The postdoctoral researchers will use the Morphicon to further our understanding of what makes morphology productive or not. This Postdoc position is part of an NWO-funded Open Competition Large project involving PI dr. Jenny Audring (Leiden University) and external partner prof. dr. Sjef Barbiers (Instituut voor de Nederlandse Taal). Tasks and responsibilities Finding, evaluating and developing digital tools, including LLMs, to assist in the morphological classification of complex words, the annotation of words and word-formation schemas, and network building; Investigating Dutch morphological constructions quantitatively and qualitatively; Presenting (joint) research results at workshops and conferences; Publishing (joint) research results ; Participating in (the organisation of) knowledge dissemination activities; Participating in meetings and training sessions of the project research group. Selection criteria: A PhD in linguistics, completed before the start date; Demonstrable expertise in computational modelling of grammatical patterns; affinity with the framework of Construction Grammar; Native or near-native proficiency of Dutch and excellent command of academic English; Excellent research and analytical skills. Terms and conditions Appointment will be according to the terms of the Collective Labour Agreement of Dutch Universities (CAO Nederlandse Universiteiten). The employment will be for a period of four years (0,75 fte) or three years (full time). You are expected to work in Leiden. The first contract will be for 12 months with an extension for the following 36 months (0,75 fte) or 24 months (full time) contingent on performance and compatibility The starting salary is € 4.925.- gross per month (pay scale 11, step 0), based on full time employment, in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Depending on qualifications, you may start at the appropriate step in scale 10 until you fully meet the requirements for scale 11 as specified by the Faculty of Humanities, particularly with regard to the number of years of relevant work experience. Leiden University offers an attractive benefits package with additional holiday (8%) and end-of-year bonuses (8.3 %), training and career development. Our individual choices model gives you some freedom to assemble your own set of terms and conditions. For international spouses we have set up a dual career programme. Candidates from outside the Netherlands may be eligible for a substantial tax break. More at https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/working-at/job-application-procedure-and-employment-conditions. Starting date: after January 2027; exact date to be determined by mutual agreement. Research at our faculty/institute Leiden University Centre for Linguistics ( LUCL ) is one of seven Institutes that falls under the Faculty of Humanities. With over 120 linguists working at the Institute, it is a rich research environment that prides itself on fostering scientific excellence. At LUCL we offer extensive research support to our researchers. LUCL has a longstanding tradition in the study of the world’s languages and features unique linguistic expertise. Researchers from our six umbrella research groups are experts in their respective fields while finding innovative ways of collaborating with colleagues in other fields and disciplines. The Faculty of Humanities at Leiden University is a unique international centre for the advanced study of languages, cultures, arts, and societies worldwide, in their historical contexts from prehistory to the present.  Our faculty is home to more than 6,000 students and 800 staff members. For more information see: https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/humanities. Diversity Fostering an inclusive community is a central element of the values and vision of Leiden University. Leiden University is committed to becoming an inclusive community which enables all students and staff to feel valued and respected and to develop their full potential. Diversity in experiences and perspectives enriches our teaching and strengthens our research. High quality teaching and research is inclusive. Questions? For further information on the research project and the Postdoc position please contact the PI, Jenny Audring, j.audring@hum.leidenuniv.nl For practical questions, please contact the Institute Manager, Maarit van Gammeren: m.s.k.van.gammeren@hum.leidenuniv.nl . Applications Applications should be submitted no later than 1 November, 2026 via the blue button in our application system. Your application should include the following information: Curriculum Vitae and a list of publications. A cover letter explaining your motivation, background and qualifications for the position (max. 2 pages). Names, positions, and email addresses of two referees (no reference letters at this point). The vacancy closes on 1 November 2026. Interviews will take place in the second half of November 2026. Enquiries from agencies are not appreciated. Apply now Information at a Glance Faculty / Unit: Faculty of Humanities Contact with (Name + position): Dr. Jenny Audring Contact Email address: j.audring@hum.leidenuniv.nl Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Study programmes Bachelor's programmes Master's programmes PhD programmes Education for Professionals Summer Schools Other modes of study Bachelor's Open Days Master's Open Days Admission & Application Organisation Archaeology Governance and Global Affairs Humanities Law Medicine/LUMC Science Social and Behavioural Sciences African Studies Centre Leiden Honours Academy ICLON (Graduate School of Teaching) About us Working at Leiden University Support Leiden University Alumni Impact Leiden-Delft-Erasmus Universities Locations Disclaimer Cookies Privacy Contact × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data center \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary)  prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Functional Cookies These cookies provide a better customer experience on this site, such as by remembering your login details, optimizing video performance, or providing us with information about how our site is used. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionalities that these third-parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Functional Cookies Show More Details Functional Cookies Provider Description Enabled YouTube YouTube is a video-sharing service where users can create their own profile, upload videos, watch, like, and comment on videos. Opting out of YouTube cookies will disable your ability to watch or interact with YouTube videos. Cookie Policy Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider YouTube Confirm My Choices Accept All Cookies ​ ​"
